---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { version } from '../lib/buildpacks';

const builder = `ghcr.io/taha2samy-3/fck-fips/builder-nodejs:${version}`;
const steps = [
  ['Detect', 'BP_OPENSSL_FIPS=true makes openssl-fips pass detection, and makes node-engine require it. A builder without openssl-fips fails here instead of building an image without the module.'],
  ['Install the module', 'openssl-fips downloads openssl-provider-fips-3.1.2 from taha2samy-3.github.io/node/apk and checks it against the signed index (embedded key), its control checksum and its data hash. fips.so and fipsmodule.cnf are used exactly as built; nothing edits them.'],
  ['Configure OpenSSL', 'A generated openssl.cnf loads only the FIPS and base providers, sets default_properties = fips=yes, requires TLS 1.2 or later with key exchange on P-256, P-384 or P-521, and turns provider errors into fatal errors (config_diagnostics = 1). OPENSSL_CONF and OPENSSL_MODULES point at it at launch.'],
  ['Verify at build time', 'node-engine runs the installed node under that configuration with --force-fips: crypto.getFips() must be 1, SHA-256 must work and MD5 must be refused. If not, the build fails.'],
  ['Run', 'At every start the module runs its known-answer self tests and integrity check. node starts with --force-fips; a module that fails its checks stops node from starting.'],
];
---

<Base title="FIPS" description="How the fck-fips buildpacks use the FIPS 140-3 validated OpenSSL FIPS Provider 3.1.2, and what that does and does not mean.">
  <h1>FIPS 140-3</h1>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="title">Validated, not certified</p>
    <p>
      The cryptography comes from the <strong>OpenSSL FIPS Provider 3.1.2</strong>, a <strong>FIPS 140-3 validated
      cryptographic module</strong>: <a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/certificate/4985">CMVP certificate #4985</a>.
      Validation is a property of that module.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>These buildpacks and the images they build are not FIPS certified, and no claim is made that they are.</strong>{' '}
      Say "uses a FIPS 140-3 validated cryptographic module", not "FIPS certified" or "FIPS compliant".
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="reveal">
    <h2 data-n="1">Turn it on</h2>
    <pre><code>{`pack build my-app --builder ${builder} \\
  --env BP_OPENSSL_FIPS=true`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Approved mode applies at launch. Install tools and build scripts often use MD5 or MD4 for non-security hashing
      (webpack 4, some caches), so the build itself runs without it unless you add <code>BP_OPENSSL_FIPS_BUILD=true</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <h2 data-n="2">What happens</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      {steps.map(([title, text], i) => (
        <li class="panel" style={`--i:${i}`}>
          <span class="label">Step {i + 1}</span>
          <h3>{title}</h3>
          <p>{text}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <h2 data-n="3">Check a running container</h2>
    <pre><code>{`docker run --rm --entrypoint launcher my-app -- node -p "require('crypto').getFips()"          # 1
docker run --rm --entrypoint launcher my-app -- node -e "require('crypto').createHash('md5')"  # error: unsupported
docker inspect my-app --format '{{ index .Config.Labels "io.fckfips.openssl-fips.certificate" }}'  # #4985`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Commands go through the launcher, which sets up FIPS mode: <code>docker run my-app node ...</code> would pass{' '}
      <code>node ...</code> as arguments to the app's start command. The image also carries{' '}
      <code>io.fckfips.openssl-fips.module</code> and <code>io.fckfips.openssl-fips.package</code> labels, and the
      provider layer has its own SBOM.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <h2 data-n="4">What your code is responsible for</h2>
    <p>
      FIPS mode cannot be switched off from inside the container: <code>crypto.setFips(false)</code>, an empty or
      replaced <code>NODE_OPTIONS</code>, <code>OPENSSL_CONF</code> or <code>OPENSSL_MODULES</code>, and{' '}
      <code>--no-force-fips</code> all leave it on, worker threads and child <code>node</code> processes inherit it, and a
      changed <code>fips.so</code> stops node from starting. MD5, MD4, RIPEMD-160, DES and Triple-DES, RC4, Blowfish,
      ChaCha20, Camellia, scrypt, Ed25519, Ed448, secp256k1 and RSA keys under 2048 bits are refused. What the
      configuration cannot do:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>X25519 and X448 still work when asked for by name.</strong> The 3.1.2 module offers them, but its
        Security Policy lists them as non-approved. TLS is restricted to the P-256, P-384 and P-521 curves, so they are
        not negotiated by default; do not set <code>ecdhCurve: 'X25519'</code> or generate <code>x25519</code> keys.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>PBKDF2 minimums are not enforced.</strong> <code>crypto.pbkdf2</code> uses OpenSSL's PKCS#5 interface,
        which skips the module's SP 800-132 checks. Use a salt of at least 16 bytes, at least 1,000 iterations (far more
        in practice) and an output of at least 14 bytes, and only for storage applications.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Only OpenSSL is covered.</strong> Cryptography that does not call <code>node:crypto</code> bypasses the
        module: native addons with their own code (<code>bcrypt</code>, <code>argon2</code>, <code>sodium-native</code>)
        and pure JavaScript libraries (<code>bcryptjs</code>, <code>crypto-js</code>, <code>tweetnacl</code>,{' '}
        <code>@noble/*</code>). bcrypt and Argon2 are not FIPS-approved; PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256 is the approved choice for
        password hashing.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <h2 data-n="5">Operational environment</h2>
    <p>
      A FIPS 140-3 certificate lists the operational environments the module was tested on. For #4985 those are
      Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 11, FreeBSD 13.1, Windows 10 and macOS 11 on x86_64, and macOS 11 on Apple M1. Wolfi is not
      among them, and Linux on ARM is not tested at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      Running the unchanged module on Wolfi is a port to an untested environment under FIPS 140-3 Implementation
      Guidance 2.3.A: the CMVP makes no statement about it, and the operator affirms that the module is used unchanged.
      That applies to linux/amd64 and, more strongly, to linux/arm64. Whether this is acceptable is your organisation's
      decision.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <h2 data-n="6">How the module package is built</h2>
    <p>
      The provider package is built in <a href="https://github.com/taha2samy-3/node">taha2samy-3/node</a> with melange,
      following section 11.1 of the security policy: the openssl-3.1.2 source named in the policy, checked against its
      SHA-256; <code>./Configure enable-fips</code>, <code>make</code>, <code>make install_fips</code> with no other options
      or patches; <code>fipsmodule.cnf</code> written only by <code>openssl fipsinstall -pedantic</code>; <code>fips.so</code>{' '}
      never stripped. Packages are signed, immutable once published, and change only when NIST lists a new certificate.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .steps { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 1.6rem; counter-reset: step; }
  .steps li { padding-top: 1.6rem; }
  .steps h3 { margin-top: 0; }
</style>
